WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.412
GROUP BY defines the grain of the summary result
Grain is simply

00:00:04.438 --> 00:00:09.575
what one output row represents At detail grain one
row might represent

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.625
one admission If you are returning individual
admission records no grouping is

00:00:14.638 --> 00:00:20.638
needed At category grain one row could represent one
diagnosis Then

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:24.912
GROUP BY diagnosis tells PostgreSQL to place rows
with the same diagnosis

00:00:24.938 --> 00:00:29.850
into the same group before calculating aggregate
values A multi-column grain is

00:00:29.862 --> 00:00:35.400
more detailed If we group by subject_id and
diagnosis one output

00:00:35.425 --> 00:00:40.550
row represents one patient-and-diagnosis combination
This is why adding a GROUP BY

00:00:40.575 --> 00:00:45.125
column usually increases the number of result rows
You are dividing the

00:00:45.138 --> 00:00:49.638
source data into smaller groups A useful beginner
rule is that any

00:00:49.662 --> 00:00:53.200
selected column that is not inside an aggregate
function usually belongs in

00:00:53.212 --> 00:00:57.838
GROUP BY PostgreSQL enforces this rule because it
needs to know which

00:00:57.862 --> 00:01:02.762
single value should represent each group Before
writing the query try saying

00:01:02.788 --> 00:01:07.012
the grain aloud If you can clearly say one row per
diagnosis

00:01:07.162 --> 00:01:10.212
or one row per patient and diagnosis then you have a
strong

00:01:10.238 --> 00:01:12.412
starting point for choosing the GROUP BY columns
